-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.21 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * NVIDIA's i386 libraries install by themselves after the apt run that needs them (ORDER 363b; Daniel, 2026-10-23: "user has not to configure, out of the box full experience, including the drivers needed to"). When dpkg's post-invoke hook finds them missing inside an apt run (apt holds dpkg's frontend lock for its whole run and says so to dpkg with DPKG_FRONTEND_LOCKED, apt-pkg/deb/dpkgpm.cc:2057), it starts sparky-stereo-hardware-nvidia-i386.service without waiting for it (systemctl --no-block) and tells the run's terminal. The unit waits until that run has ended (apt-get check with DPkg::Lock::Timeout=-1, apt-pkg/deb/debsystem.cc:80-116), asks apt whether it can install them (apt-get --simulate without the locks), installs nvidia-driver-libs:i386 and libcuda1:i386 at the driver's version under the engine (sparky-stereo-problems run) and keeps a notice for this boot, which the session's notifier shows. The person's `dpkg --add-architecture i386; apt-get update; apt-get install steam-installer` now ends with them installed, and that run never waits on the unit
  * After a dpkg run of its own (dpkg --add-architecture i386, dpkg -i) nothing is started: its hooks run with dpkg's locks already free (measured), so a unit would run at once, beside the apt-get update the person runs next, which takes only the lists lock and never waits for it (apt-pkg/acquire.cc:180; measured: exit 100 after 1 s even with DPkg::Lock::Timeout=-1). The problem is kept as before, and the terminal is told that the next apt run brings them
  * The problem and its Repair stay for a failure, with the reason: apt finds no way to install them (that version gone, i386's package lists not read yet: the simulation's output is kept), or the installation fails (no network: apt's output is kept); where no unit can be started (systemd not running, a chroot, policy-rc.d forbidding it, the unit masked), the problem says which. The unit's failure is told once, by the engine's record with its Repair (a drop-in empties the engine's handler for every failed service for this unit). A stop while the unit waits (a shutdown) ends the wait and keeps the problem; a stop while it installs lets the installation finish, as Debian's apt-daily-upgrade.service does
  * The unit installs nothing that an apt run brought already (Hardware Setup's own setup installs them with the driver), and runs no apt-get update, which would race the person's own

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.20 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * NVIDIA's i386 libraries when i386 comes after NVIDIA's driver (ORDER 363; Daniel, 2026-11-01: "either it works or not and the user must know"): Hardware Setup installs nvidia-driver-libs:i386 and libcuda1:i386 with the driver where dpkg already has i386, but nothing installed them when i386 was enabled later (dpkg --add-architecture i386, as Steam's and Wine's instructions say), since Debian's steam-libs only suggests them and apt adds no recommendation to a package installed before. Now dpkg's post-invoke hook (/etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/sparky-stereo-hardware) checks after every dpkg run, dpkg --add-architecture i386 among them: where NVIDIA's driver is installed for amd64 and dpkg has i386 but the two i386 libraries are not installed at the driver's version, the edition's engine keeps the problem "NVIDIA's 32-bit libraries are missing" (its notifier shows it at login, its Repair installs them) and the terminal of that dpkg or apt run is told, in the nine languages; when they are there again, or i386 or the driver goes, the problem is cleared. A dpkg hook cannot install them itself: dpkg and apt hold their locks until their run ends
  * sparky-stereo-hardware --nvidia-i386, the problem's Repair: installs nvidia-driver-libs:i386 and libcuda1:i386 at the installed driver's version (615 or 580), after apt-get update (the i386 indices arrive with it after dpkg --add-architecture), and says what failed when apt cannot

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.19 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * NVIDIA's 580 path installs NVIDIA's 580 user space from the edition's repository: NVIDIA publishes it for Debian only in its repository for Debian 12, whose key Debian 14's APT refuses, so the edition carries NVIDIA's own packages, their files byte for byte, at its 580 module's version, and the setup names nvidia-driver and libnvcuvid1 at that version (580.196.07-1+sparky2+lc1). Before (0.1.18, not published), the path stopped at its simulation because nothing apt reads had them (ORDER 354 milestone 2; Daniel, 2026-11-01: "why can't we have our own, if nvidia's is not compatible?")
  * Where dpkg has i386 (dpkg --add-architecture i386, which Steam and 32-bit Wine need), both NVIDIA paths also install nvidia-driver-libs:i386 and libcuda1:i386 at the driver's version, NVIDIA's i386 OpenGL, Vulkan and CUDA libraries, which NVIDIA's amd64 packages recommend; they are named, so a downgrade or a reinstall brings them too. Before, nothing installed them: apt offered NVIDIA's i386 615 libraries already (NVIDIA's CUDA repository is flat, one index for every architecture, and the Architectures: amd64 of the edition's source does not limit it; measured with apt 3.3.3), but the setup named none, and apt adds no recommendation to a package installed before i386 was there, so 32-bit games got no NVIDIA OpenGL or Vulkan on either path. The dry run says so
  * The 580 path installs sparky-stereo-cuda-repo with its lock, in the same first transaction, as the 615 path does: NVIDIA's 580 nvidia-driver-libs depends on libnvidia-egl-xcb1 and libnvidia-egl-xlib1 (>= 1:1.0.1), NVIDIA's epoch, which only NVIDIA's CUDA repository has (Debian has 1.0.3-1, without it). 0.1.18 installed no CUDA repository on that path, so it worked only where the image had already installed the package (measured in a container without it: "nvidia-driver-libs Depends libnvidia-egl-xcb1 (>= 1:1.0.1) but none of the choices are installable"). The 615 lock that package depends on decides nothing while the 580 lock is installed; the setup installs no CUDA toolkit (CUDA 13 does not run on these cards)
  * Supersedes both packages numbered 0.1.18, which can never sit in one repository: ORDER 358's (in the round's cohort until this one is accepted) and ORDER 354's (not published); the changes of both are in this version, their lines below

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.18 (2026-11-02)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 358:
    * Report unreadable startkderc or failed systemdBoot reads in the shared problems
      engine. Do not apply the session zink override without a successful read; keep
      the desktop shell running and explain how to repair and start a new session.
  * ORDER 354:
    * Maxwell, Pascal and Volta cards get NVIDIA's driver: NVIDIA's 580 branch, the last for them, with the edition's build of its kernel modules for the edition's kernels (nvidia-legacy-580xx-kernel-dkms) and its own driver lock (sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock), picked by PCI ID from the "legacy_580.xx" table of NVIDIA's 615.71.09 README (share/nvidia-legacy-580xx.ids). Before, these cards got no driver offer and kept nouveau (ORDER 354; Daniel, 2026-11-01: "why can't we have our own, if nvidia's is not compatible?")
    * The plan window shows both drivers for such a card, in the person's language: NVIDIA's 580 driver (the default: full clocks, hardware video decoding, CUDA 12; no HDMI 3D modes, since NVIDIA's display code is closed) and nouveau (the edition's HDMI 3D modes, at the card's boot clocks, video decoded in software), with the buttons "Install matched support" and "Install matched support, keep nouveau"; --nvidia edition (the default) and --nvidia nouveau choose the same way in a terminal
    * The 580 path installs its lock first, in a transaction of its own, then reads the repositories, checks the transaction and installs nvidia-driver, libnvcuvid1 (both at the module's NVIDIA version), the module and nvidia-vaapi-driver, letting apt take a newer NVIDIA user space back to 580; it installs no CUDA repository (CUDA 13 does not run on these cards) and no runtime power management (it needs a Turing card or newer). Each failure stops it with an ERROR line in the nine languages, and nouveau stays only as the person's choice
    * The open modules on a machine that had the 580 driver: the 580 lock and module are purged first, since that lock decides NVIDIA's packages before the 615 lock while it is installed
    * A card the edition does not drive says why in the plan: Kepler and older name the NVIDIA legacy branch that last ran them (share/nvidia-legacy-older.tsv), which the edition does not build; a card in none of NVIDIA's tables says so; several NVIDIA cards that need different drivers keep nouveau, as before
    * Tests: tests/nvidia-branches.py with fixtures of a Turing, a Pascal, a Maxwell laptop, a Kepler and an unknown card; detection and action cases for the 580 path

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.16 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * No fallback to nouveau: when the edition's NVIDIA module is not in the repositories, its version names no NVIDIA version, the lock and the CUDA repository package cannot be installed, apt update fails or the preflight fails, the NVIDIA setup stops with an ERROR line that says what failed, why and what to do, purges what it installed, and the setup exits non-zero. Before, each of these logged "FALLBACK ... nouveau firmware installed" and the setup reported success (Daniel, 2026-11-01: "nothing we do should silently fail or have a fall back"; ORDER 343)
  * nouveau on a supported NVIDIA card is the person's own choice: the plan window's new button "Install matched support, keep nouveau", or --nvidia nouveau
  * Every step says when it fails and the run ends with the list of errors and exit status 1: package installs (before, a failed one stopped the run without a word), the missing firmware, the power-profiles-daemon actions the machine does not offer (before, logged as UNSUPPORTED and counted as done), the wheel drivers (before, three silent refusals), the OpenGL stereo default, and any unexpected failure, with its line
  * When the menu started the setup, the list of errors also opens in a window before the terminal closes, each error in the person's language: the error messages are numbered strings (LOCAL29 to LOCAL54) in the nine language files, like the window's labels, and the log keeps every ERROR line in English for support. The plan window's own failure is said instead of being taken for Cancel
  * An unexpected failure inside a step (set -E) is said with its line and command too; before, set -e ended the setup there without a word
  * Recommends sparky-stereo-cuda-repo, so an installed system takes the narrowed CUDA pin and the edition's dkms with an ordinary upgrade (installed systems take Recommends by default), and no file of the image's is left to decide
  * A CUDA repository the person set up already is kept and named, and sparky-stereo-cuda-repo comes with the lock anyway: from 20261101.2 it writes no source of its own when another source names NVIDIA's URI, and its pins go by origin, so they apply to the person's source too. Before (0.1.15), only the lock was added there, and NVIDIA's packages kept the person's pins
  * French: the plan window's button reads "Installer la prise en charge correspondante, garder nouveau"
  * The dry run says what would stop the NVIDIA setup instead of ending without a word when apt does not know the module; a loader that does not list its CPU levels is noted; the postinst says when the OpenGL stereo default could not be written
  * The plan says why an unsupported NVIDIA card keeps nouveau
  * Supersedes 0.1.15, which was not published; its changes are in this version

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.15 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * NVIDIA's CUDA repository comes from the edition's package sparky-stereo-cuda-repo (its source, NVIDIA's key and the edition's pins), installed with sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock in a transaction of their own before the driver, so the lock and the pins are in force when apt chooses NVIDIA's user space. Before, the setup downloaded NVIDIA's cuda-keyring, wrote the source and a pin of every NVIDIA package at 1002 itself, and the lock came only as the module's dependency, in the driver's own transaction (ORDER 338, ORDER 329's findings)
  * A CUDA repository the person set up already, such as NVIDIA's own cuda-keyring, is kept as it is, and only the lock is added: two sources for NVIDIA's URI with different key files stop apt
  * A failed install of the repository package or a failed preflight purges what the run installed
  * The NVIDIA driver version is read from a module built through Lean Code too: the published nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d is 615.71.09-2+stereo3d7+lc1, which the old pattern (+stereo3dN at the end) did not match, so the setup fell back to nouveau with "edition NVIDIA module has no NVIDIA version" and the dry run printed no version
  * share/cuda-key.sh moved to sparky-stereo-cuda-repo's recipe

